{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789832184,"nonce":"1jqcoTaUd0Nd9lfSFhjXterO4oE=","title":"Int into an Int64 local a lambda captured stores 32 bits; a Dynamic-typed scalar register is passed raw; a boxed Null\u003cInt\u003e reaches a scalar extern parameter as a pointer","message":"Three call-boundary and store-boundary representation gaps found while making TestInt64 run:\n\n1. `var a:Int64; a = make(10, 5); a = 1;` with `a` captured by a later bind: the cell store wrote the Int as 32 bits (10\u003c\u003c32 | 1 read back). The local's declared type resolved to the class under the abstract (`___Int64`), so `maybe_abstract_from_convert` found no abstract and no `@:from ofInt`. Int64 is the native i64: an integer register bound for any Int64 spelling is widened.\n\n2. `a.high` on an Int64 is typed `Int32`, an abstract whose module lowers late, so the typer left the property `Dynamic`; `eq(a.high, 0)` then passed the raw I32 register into a Dynamic slot: 0 printed as null, 0x80000000 was dereferenced (SIGSEGV). A scalar register bound for a Dynamic slot is boxed by its register type when the typer said Dynamic.\n\n3. `Int64.ofInt(d)` with `d = s.charCodeAt(i) - '0'.code` (a `Null\u003cInt\u003e` local, boxed at declaration) passed the box pointer to the I32 parameter; `parseString(\"-23\")` returned -505775806688. `maybe_box_for_extern_call` now unboxes a pointer register for a scalar parameter. The register decides, not the HIR type: a `Null\u003cInt\u003e` temporary is still raw bits.\n\nAlso: `resolve_abstract_conversion_function` looked the @:from method up under `rayzor.\u003cName\u003e` only; the abstract's qualified name (`haxe.Int64`) is tried first, and the wrapper's own signature is used.","files":null}]}